{
  "analysis": "Notice that base_mean is IDENTICAL! 25.05, 90.27, 43.38, 115.97! But log2fc and pvalue are completely different! Why? Look at base_mean: base_mean is the mean of normalized counts across all samples. Since base_mean is identical, `de_draft.tsv` was computed on the EXACT SAME count matrix `gene_counts_merged.tsv`! But why is log2fc different? What differed in the model or sample sheet or condition? Wait! Look at `samples.tsv`:\nRS19 treated\nRS22 vehicle\nRS26 vehicle\nRS27 vehicle\nRS47 treated\nRS62 vehicle\nRS69 vehicle\nRS75 treated\nRS79 treated\nRS80 treated\nRS89 treated\nRS97 vehicle\nWait! Were the conditions swapped or different? Or did `de_draft.tsv` use something else?",
  "plan": "Investigate why log2fc in `de_draft.tsv` differs from `test_de.tsv`. Let's write a python script to check possible reasons (condition coding, sample subset, etc.).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"
import pandas as pd, numpy as np

draft = pd.read_csv('/task/input/previous_analysis/de_draft.tsv', sep='\\t')
test = pd.read_csv('/tmp/test_de.tsv', sep='\\t')
print('Draft log2fc vs test log2fc correlation:', draft['log2fc'].corr(test['log2fc']))
\" \n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Look at the ratio: 14841 / 45195 = 0.328377. Let's see if df1['RS79'] is exactly from the first N reads of RS79.fastq.gz, or if RS79 was interrupted or truncated or subsampled! Let's check `matrix_all_reads` requirement: `matrix_all_reads`: 'Every column of gene_counts_merged.tsv was obtained from all reads of a FASTQ file, not from a part of a file.' Clearly RS79 was NOT obtained from all reads of RS79.fastq.gz! That means `matrix_all_reads` is `defective`! Now what about `matrix_sample_files`? 'Every column of gene_counts_merged.tsv was obtained from the FASTQ file that samples.tsv lists for the sample in the column header.' Did all 12 columns come from the fastq file listed in samples.tsv (even if RS79 was partial)? Or was RS79 from a different file? Let's check if RS79 df1 is a head of RS79.fastq.gz.",
  "plan": "Check if quantifying the first N reads of RS79.fastq.gz matches df1['RS79'].",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"
import gzip
# Total lines in RS79.fastq.gz was 376360 -> 94090 reads.
# If ratio is ~0.33, maybe 30000 reads or 100000 lines?
# Let's test with salmon quant on a truncated RS79.fastq
\"\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "zcat /task/input/fastq/RS79.fastq.gz | head -n 120000 | gzip > /tmp/rs79_test.fastq.gz && salmon quant -i /tmp/prev_quant/index -l SR -r /tmp/rs79_test.fastq.gz --validateMappings -p 2 -o /tmp/rs79_test_quant > /dev/null 2>&1 && python3 -c \"import pandas as pd; sf = pd.read_csv('/tmp/rs79_test_quant/quant.sf', sep='\\t'); t2g = pd.read_csv('/task/input/reference/tx2gene.tsv', sep='\\t', dtype=str); g = dict(zip(t2g['transcript_id'], t2g['gene_id'])); sf_g = sf.groupby(sf['Name'].map(g))['NumReads'].sum(); df1 = pd.read_csv('/task/input/previous_analysis/gene_counts_merged.tsv', sep='\\t', index_col=0); print('Diff with 30k reads:', (df1['RS79'] - sf_g).abs().max())\"\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The IFS=$'\\t' in the bash while loop got stripped or had an empty delimiter because of quoting. Let's write a python script to run salmon quantification for all samples cleanly.",
  "plan": "Write a python script to run salmon quant for each sample in samples.tsv.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 -c \"
import subprocess, pandas as pd
samples = pd.read_csv('/task/input/samples.tsv', sep='\\t')
for _, row in samples.iterrows():
    sid = row['sample_id']
    fq = row['fastq_file']
    cmd = ['salmon', 'quant', '-i', '/tmp/my_quant/index', '-l', 'U', '-r', f'/task/input/fastq/{fq}', '--validateMappings', '-p', '4', '-o', f'/tmp/my_quant/quant/{sid}']
    print('Quantifying', sid)
    subprocess.run(cmd, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, check=True)
print('Done all')
\"\n",
      "duration": 15.0
    }
  ]
}
